CH = dict(num=2, tag="Economics", theme="Demand, supply, equilibrium and government intervention",
 title="The Price Puzzle: What Drives the Market",
 intro="Mango prices fall mid-season, hotel rooms cost more at New Year, and masks became costly in 2020. Prices are not random: they respond to two forces, demand and supply, and sometimes to the government. This chapter explains how.",
 big_questions=["What factors influence the demand for and supply of goods and services in a market?", "How are prices determined through the interaction of demand and supply?", "What is market equilibrium, and does it exist in the real world?", "How and why does the government intervene in the market?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Why Do Prices Change?"),
 ("p", "Prices react to what people want, how much is available, seasons, festivals, trends and even rumours. For every good or service, from snacks to mobile phones, price is decided by **demand** and **supply** acting together. In Grade 7 you saw that sellers adjust prices that are too high or too low; this chapter looks at the forces behind that."),
 ("h2", "Demand"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Demand** is the quantity of a product that people are **willing and able** to buy at a particular price. Desire alone is not demand: it must come with **purchasing power** (how much one unit of a currency can buy)."),
 ("h3", "Law of Demand"),
 ("ul", ["When the price of a good rises, quantity demanded **falls**; when price falls, quantity demanded **rises**.", "This **inverse relationship** between price and quantity demanded is the Law of Demand (mangoes bought in smaller quantities at the start of the season, in larger quantities as prices fall)."]),
 ("h3", "Individual demand: the demand schedule and curve"),
 ("p", "**Individual demand** is the quantity one consumer wants to buy at different prices, with other factors constant. The table of prices and quantities is the **demand schedule**; drawing it on a graph gives the **demand curve**."),
 ("table", ["Price of mangoes (₹/kg)", "Quantity demanded by Srivalli", "Point on graph"], [["150", "1 kg", "A"], ["100", "2 kg", "B"], ["50", "3 kg", "C"]]),
 ("ul", ["Price goes on the y-axis and quantity on the x-axis. Joining A, B, C gives a **downward-sloping line DD'**, the demand curve, which shows the inverse price–quantity relation, assuming income, taste etc. are constant."]),
 ("h3", "Market demand"),
 ("p", "**Market demand** is the total quantity demanded by all potential buyers at each price, i.e. the **sum of all individual demands**."),
 ("table", ["Price", "Srivalli (Q1)", "Alex (Q2)", "Israt (Q3)", "Market demand (QD = Q1+Q2+Q3)"], [["₹150", "1 kg", "2 kg", "3 kg", "6 kg"], ["₹100", "2 kg", "4 kg", "6 kg", "12 kg"], ["₹50", "3 kg", "6 kg", "9 kg", "18 kg"]]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "The market demand curve is **flatter** than an individual curve because many buyers respond to the same price change together. When price falls from ₹150 to ₹50, Srivalli buys 2 kg more, but the market buys 12 kg more."),
 ("h3", "Other determinants of demand"),
 ("p", "Demand can change even when price does not, as with the rush for a new smartphone. The main factors are:"),
 ("table", ["Determinant", "How it changes demand", "Example"], [
  ["Price of related goods: **substitutes**", "Goods that replace each other: if one becomes dearer, demand for the other rises", "Tea and coffee; mangoes and bananas"],
  ["Price of related goods: **complements**", "Goods used together move together: demand for one rises when the other's demand rises", "Smartphones and earphones; cars and petrol; printers and cartridges; cinema tickets and popcorn"],
  ["Income of the consumer", "Higher income means people buy more or pick better quality even at the same price", "Moving to branded products"],
  ["Taste and preference", "Personal liking decides demand", "Srivalli likes mangoes and will not switch to cheaper oranges"],
  ["Population size and composition", "More people raise demand; age mix shapes the type of goods", "More children: sports shoes; more working adults: formal shoes; more elderly: orthopaedic shoes. India's large population drives domestic demand"],
  ["Seasonality", "Weather, festivals and culture shift demand through the year", "Books at the start of the session, sweets at festivals, sweaters in winter"],
  ["Future price expectations", "If prices are expected to fall, buyers wait; if to rise, they buy now", "Postponing durable purchases before Diwali or New Year for discounts"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Diminishing marginal utility:** the extra satisfaction from each additional unit of a good keeps falling (the first mango is delicious, the fifth hardly interesting). Lower utility means lower willingness to pay, so demand falls."),
 ("h2", "Supply"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Supply** is the quantity of a product that sellers are **willing and able** to offer at a particular price."),
 ("h3", "Law of Supply"),
 ("ul", ["As price rises, quantity supplied **rises**; as price falls, it **falls** (a **direct relationship**).", "Reason: higher prices mean higher profit, which encourages producers to produce more and attracts new firms."]),
 ("p", "At the start of the mango season supply is low and mangoes are costly; mid-season supply rises and price falls. **When supply is less than demand, prices rise; when supply exceeds demand, prices fall.**"),
 ("h3", "Individual and market supply"),
 ("table", ["Price", "Seller A", "Seller B", "Seller C", "Market supply (A+B+C = QS)"], [["₹50", "1 kg", "3 kg", "2 kg", "6 kg"], ["₹100", "2 kg", "4 kg", "6 kg", "12 kg"], ["₹150", "3 kg", "7 kg", "8 kg", "18 kg"]]),
 ("ul", ["Individual supply is what one seller offers at different prices; **market supply** is the sum of all sellers' supplies.", "Plotting price against quantity gives an **upward-sloping supply curve** (SS' for one seller, SMSM' for the market)."]),
 ("h3", "Other determinants of supply"),
 ("table", ["Determinant", "Effect", "Example"], [
  ["Price of related goods", "Producers shift to the more profitable alternative", "Wheat price low and chickpea price high: the farmer plants more chickpeas next season"],
  ["Number of sellers", "More sellers mean more competition and higher market supply, pulling prices down; fewer sellers do the opposite", "A market flooded with sellers"],
  ["Technology", "Better technology lowers the cost of production and raises supply (and the reverse)", "Drip irrigation and weather sensors raise crop output; cold storage helps transport"],
  ["Future expectations", "Expected boom in demand raises output; expected fall lowers it; sellers may hold back stock", "Potato wholesalers holding stock to sell at higher peak-season prices"],
  ["Other factors", "Cost of inputs, discovery of alternative inputs, depletion of resources, weather and disasters change supply", "Drought reduces crop supply"]]),
 ("h2", "Market Equilibrium"),
 ("p", "A market is a negotiation between what buyers are willing to pay and what sellers are willing to accept, so prices come from the interaction of demand and supply."),
 ("table", ["Price (₹)", "Quantity demanded (kg)", "Quantity supplied (kg)", "Outcome"], [["40", "38", "6", "Qs < Qd: **excess demand** (shortage)"], ["100", "12", "12", "Qs = Qd: **market equilibrium**"], ["150", "8", "43", "Qs > Qd: **excess supply** (surplus)"]]),
 ("ul", ["At ₹100 quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. This is **market equilibrium**: equilibrium price = ₹100, equilibrium quantity = 12 kg. The market is 'cleared': no shortage, no surplus, no pressure on price.", "On the graph, equilibrium is the point **E** where the demand curve DMDM' intersects the supply curve SMSM'.", "At a price below equilibrium there is excess demand and price tends to rise; above equilibrium there is excess supply and price tends to fall, so the market moves toward equilibrium."]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Market equilibrium** is the point where demand equals supply, with neither excess supply (surplus) nor excess demand (shortage); prices stay stable unless external factors change."),
 ("h3", "Does equilibrium exist in the real world?"),
 ("ul", ["In theory equilibrium is a fixed intersection point, but real markets are **dynamic**: technology, wages, interest rates, wars, political events, pandemics, weather and disasters keep shifting demand and supply.", "So equilibrium is never stable; the market is always adjusting to a **new** equilibrium.", "**COVID-19 example (2020):** demand for face masks surged, supply could not catch up quickly and prices rose; later suppliers adjusted and prices fell; after the pandemic demand dropped and prices returned to earlier levels."]),
 ("h3", "Hotel tariffs: a dynamic market"),
 ("table", ["Situation (hotel with 100 rooms in Goa)", "Tariff per night"], [["Off-season weekday (Monday in July)", "₹1,500"], ["Weekend in tourist season (Saturday in December)", "₹8,000"], ["New Year's Eve (very high demand)", "₹25,000"], ["Group tour cancels", "Tariff may be cut by 40 per cent overnight to fill rooms"]]),
 ("p", "Hotels may change tariff several times a day to maximise **revenue** (total money earned from sales before expenses). They watch booking speed, rival hotels' tariffs, festivals or conferences in the area, weather forecasts, days left before arrival and past booking trends."),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Choices made today affect future supply: fast-fashion demand, overfishing and overuse of groundwater can reduce future supply. Short-term gain must be balanced with long-term sustainability, which also shapes equilibrium."),
 ("h2", "Role of Government in the Economy"),
 ("p", "India is a market-based but **regulated** economy, and the fourth-largest economy in the world. Markets allocate goods by willingness and ability to pay, which may leave poor people unable to afford essentials such as medicines, so fairness and equity need the government's attention."),
 ("h3", "1. Regulation of unfair practices"),
 ("ul", ["The government protects consumers, workers and producers from exploitation. A **price ceiling** is a maximum price (for example for medicines) to stop overcharging. A **price floor** is a minimum price (for example a minimum wage); to be effective a price floor must be set **above** the equilibrium price.", "**Monopoly** (one seller, or a few, controlling supply with no close substitutes) allows higher prices, poorer quality and restricted supply; the government keeps price and quantity in check.", "Regulators ensure transparency: **RBI** (banking), **Central Consumer Protection Authority** (consumer rights and unfair trade), **TRAI** (telecom), **SEBI** (securities market)."]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Sanitiser case (COVID-19):** demand surged, stocks ran out and prices soared; some shopkeepers resorted to **hoarding** and **black marketing**. The government declared sanitisers essential commodities under the **Essential Commodities Act, 1955** and capped the price at ₹100 for a 200 ml bottle. Production then rose and supply reached fair prices. Such controls suit emergencies, not forever."),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning"], [["Hoarding", "Accumulating goods beyond immediate need, driven by fear of shortage or expected price rise"], ["Black marketing", "Illegal trade in goods and services that are banned or regulated"], ["Price ceiling", "Maximum price a seller can charge"], ["Price floor", "Minimum price that can be charged"], ["Monopoly", "Single seller controlling the whole supply of a product with no close substitute"]]),
 ("h3", "2. Provision of public goods"),
 ("ul", ["**Public goods** (roads, bridges, parks, street lights, national defence, sanitation and drainage) benefit all citizens and are usually not offered by private firms because they bring no direct profit.", "**Free-rider problem:** if each family is asked to contribute ₹5,000 to build a park, many think others will pay and they can still use it, so too little money is collected and the park is never built. Hence the government must provide or fund such goods for welfare, development and equal access."]),
 ("h3", "Limitations of government intervention"),
 ("table", ["Problem", "Explanation", "Example"], [
  ["Price distortions and reduced producer incentives", "Prices fixed below market level reduce the motive to supply, causing shortages", "Maximum wheat price of ₹20/kg when the market price is ₹30/kg"],
  ["Compliance burden", "Too many licences, permits and rules hurt small firms and the **ease of doing business**", "A small restaurant needing food, fire, pollution and local clearances"],
  ["Discourages innovation and entrepreneurship", "Price controls and heavy regulation reduce the return on investing in new ideas", "Farmers not investing in better seeds or irrigation"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Ease of doing business** measures how simple it is to start, run and close a business, depending on bureaucratic control, efficiency and legal frameworks. A democratic government is accountable to the people and must balance the voices of consumers, producers and workers while intervening."),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is demand? How does it differ from desire?", "Demand is the quantity people are willing and able to buy at a price. Desire without purchasing power is not demand."),
  ("State the Law of Demand.", "Other things constant, quantity demanded falls when price rises and rises when price falls: an inverse relationship."),
  ("Why is the demand curve downward sloping?", "Higher price lowers quantity demanded and lower price raises it; it shows the inverse relation."),
  ("Distinguish individual demand from market demand.", "Individual demand is one buyer's quantity at various prices; market demand is the sum of all buyers' demand."),
  ("Name four determinants of demand other than price.", "Price of related goods, income, taste and preference, seasonality, population, future price expectations."),
  ("Distinguish substitutes from complements.", "Substitutes replace each other (tea, coffee); complements are used together (smartphone, earphones)."),
  ("State the Law of Supply and give reasons.", "Quantity supplied rises with price because higher prices bring more profit and attract new firms."),
  ("List factors that determine supply.", "Price of related goods, number of sellers, technology, future expectations, input costs, weather."),
  ("What is market equilibrium?", "The price and quantity at which demand equals supply, with no shortage or surplus. In the table: ₹100 and 12 kg."),
  ("What happens at a price above equilibrium?", "Excess supply (surplus), so price tends to fall toward equilibrium."),
  ("Why is equilibrium never stable in the real world?", "Technology, weather, wars, pandemics and other events keep changing demand and supply (masks in 2020, hotel tariffs)."),
  ("Why does the government intervene in markets?", "To prevent exploitation, control monopolies and hoarding, ensure essentials are affordable and provide public goods."),
  ("Differentiate a price ceiling from a price floor.", "Ceiling: maximum price (medicines). Floor: minimum price (minimum wage), effective only above equilibrium."),
  ("What are the drawbacks of excessive regulation?", "Distorted prices and shortages, compliance costs for small firms, and weaker innovation."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["**Demand** and **supply** together determine price.", "**Law of Demand:** price and quantity demanded move in **opposite** directions. **Law of Supply:** they move in the **same** direction.", "Market demand and market supply are **sums** of individual demand and supply.", "Demand is shifted by related goods, income, taste, population, seasons and expectations; supply by related goods, number of sellers, technology and expectations.", "**Equilibrium** is where demand equals supply (₹100, 12 kg in the mango example), but real markets keep moving.", "Governments regulate unfair practices, check monopolies, set price ceilings and floors and provide **public goods**; excessive control causes distortions."])
